      "type": "h1",
      "content": "IT Service Continuity Analysis: Q3 2025"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "content": "Prepared for: Executive Leadership Committee (ELC)\nDate: October 15, 2025\nVersion: 1.0\n\nThis analysis evaluates the resilience of core Information Technology services supporting our underwriting, claims processing, and policy administration functions. Given the increasing reliance on digital infrastructure and the critical nature of insurance claims payout cycles, maintaining service continuity is paramount to mitigating reputational and financial risk. This document outlines key findings, identifies potential Single Points of Failure (SPOFs), and proposes strategic mitigations for the upcoming fiscal period."
    },
    {
      "type": "h2",
      "content": "Executive Summary and Key Findings"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "content": "Overall system resilience remains stable (99.98% uptime average over Q2), but targeted stress testing revealed elevated risks within the third-party API integration layers and the legacy policy filing database. Specifically, the claims portal experienced intermittent latency spikes during peak simulation loads, impacting end-user experience. Management attention is required in three key areas: Disaster Recovery (DR) synchronization, vendor risk management, and cloud scalability optimization."
    },
    {
      "type": "h3",
      "content": "Observed Service Vulnerability Hotspots"
    },
    {
      "type": "ul",
      "items": [
        "**Claims Processing API:** Identified a dependency on a single regional DNS resolver, creating a potential SPOF during localized network outages.",
        "**Policy Administration System (PAS):** The integration with the legacy Core Policy Database (CPD) relies on an outdated middleware layer. The Mean Time To Recovery (MTTR) for this subsystem is estimated at 72 hours, exceeding the internal threshold of 24 hours.",
        "**Customer Portal Authentication:** While utilizing multi-factor authentication (MFA), the failover mechanism for the Identity Access Management (IAM) service requires manual intervention, significantly increasing the RTO window."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "h2",
      "content": "Operational Metrics and Performance Review"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "content": "The following table summarizes the Measured Recovery Time Objectives (RTOs) and Recovery Point Objectives (RPOs) for critical business functions against industry best practices and internal mandates. Performance benchmarks were assessed using simulated failure scenarios (Blackout Day modeling)."
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "headers": [
        "Business Function",
        "Current RTO (Hours)",
        "Target RTO (Hours)",
        "Required Action"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Claims Intake & Triage",
          "12",
          "4",
          "Accelerate DR automation for API gateways."
        ],
        [
          "Underwriting Submission",
          "24",
          "8",
          "Review bandwidth allocation and load balancing configurations."
        ],
        [
          "Policy Issuance & Billing",
          "48",
          "12",
          "Modernize middleware layer connecting to CPD (Phase II)."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "h3",
      "content": "Recommendations and Mitigation Strategy"
    },
    {
      "type": "ol",
